course: dictionary
lesson: 14980
title: Repayable money
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Repayable is an adjective about money. Money that is repayable must be paid back, usually by a certain time. «The bank lent Sam £50. The loan is repayable in ten months.» Sam has to give the money back within ten months.
explain: Look at the parts of the word. Repay means pay back, and -able means can be done. So repayable means can be paid back. You can use the clue to work out the meaning of other words with -able, like washable or readable.
explain: The money tells you the meaning. A loan is borrowed, so it must be given back. A gift is kept. «The club gave us £20 as a gift, so it is not repayable.» Check it by swapping in your meaning: could you pay this money back? No, it is yours to keep.
fact: Repayable means that money must be paid back, or can be paid back.
fact: «A repayable loan» is money you borrow and must give back, often with interest, which is extra money.
fact: «Repayable in instalments» means paid back in smaller amounts, bit by bit.
fact: «Non-repayable» means it does not have to be paid back, like a gift or a grant.
exercise: «Mia borrowed £30. The loan is repayable by June.» What does repayable mean here? | *Must be paid back by June | The money is lost in the post | Mia can paint it | Mia keeps the money as a present
exercise: «A loan of £100 is repayable in 5 equal parts.» How much is each part? | £25 | £10 | *£20 | £50
exercise: «The council gave a grant, so it is non-repayable.» What does non-repayable mean? | *It does not have to be paid back | Must be paid back by Friday | It is a very expensive loan | The name of a bank
test: «The bank offered Leo a repayable loan of £200.» What does a repayable loan mean? | A bank that has closed | A prize for winning a game | A gift that Leo keeps | *A loan that Leo must pay back
test: Which of these is NOT repayable? | *A birthday present from your gran | A loan to buy a bike that you pay back monthly | Money borrowed for a school trip that must be returned | A loan of £50 from the bank
exam: Jo borrows £60, repayable in 3 equal parts. How much is each payment? | £60 | £15 | £30 | *£20
